<speak>
<prosody rate="85%" pitch="-2%" volume="+2dB">
<mark name="stage_pre_talk"/>
<p>You made it here today. <break time="400ms"/>You are sitting comfortably. <break time="400ms"/>You can hear my voice clearly. <break time="500ms"/>And that means the part of you that wants this change is already at work. <break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>So before we begin, here is what hypnosis actually is. <break time="500ms"/>
It is a natural state — you drift in and out of it every day, in movies, in daydreams, on familiar drives. <break time="600ms"/>
It is a narrow, comfortable focus that lets the protective filter of the mind relax, so a negative pattern can be exchanged for a positive one. <break time="700ms"/>
When I say the words deep sleep, that is a cue for this relaxed state — not actual sleep. <break time="500ms"/>
You stay aware the whole time, you hear everything, you cannot get stuck, and you would never do or say anything you wouldn't do wide awake. It is not a truth serum. <break time="800ms"/>
If your mind wanders, that is normal — it will return to my voice on its own. <break time="600ms"/>
Some people feel light and tingly, some feel pleasantly heavy, some simply feel calm. Whatever you experience is right for you. <break time="700ms"/>
And you may even wonder whether you are hypnotized — that wondering is usually the sign that you are.</p>
<p>This is a do-with process, not a do-to process — your participation and imagination are the engine. <break time="600ms"/>
The reason you are here is greater day-to-day comfort and ease alongside the care plan directed by Dr. Patel, and hypnosis lets us work with the subconscious directly, so the change you have been forcing becomes far easier. <break time="700ms"/>
I will run a few tests today to learn how your mind takes in information. You cannot pass or fail them — so no helping, no hindering — they simply tell me how to work in your exact style. <break time="600ms"/>
Any questions before we start? <mark name="await_questions"/><break time="4s"/>
Good — then today we begin building the new associations that carry you to your goal.</p>
<mark name="stage_tom"/>
<p>Let me show you how the mind is organized, because it explains exactly why this works. <break time="600ms"/>
At the base sits the primitive area — pure survival instinct — with two settings: fight, which pushes through stress, and flight, the urge to withdraw and escape it. <break time="800ms"/>
From birth to about age eight, the mind accepts nearly everything as true. That is when the programming forms — some of it positive, some negative — and those experiences become the known associations: what we believe, what feels familiar. <break time="900ms"/>
Those associations make up the subconscious mind — about eighty-eight percent of your mental power. <break time="700ms"/>
As we grow, a filter forms above it — the critical mind — whose whole job is to protect the known, even when the known is the problem. <break time="700ms"/>
And on top, the conscious mind: logic, reasoning, decisions, willpower — the willpower that never quite works when you need it — only about twelve percent of the power.</p>
<p>So here is what has been happening with greater day-to-day comfort and ease alongside the care plan directed by Dr. Patel. <break time="500ms"/>
The decision to change starts in the conscious mind, travels down, hits the critical filter, and gets checked against the old programming. <break time="600ms"/>
The programming resists, the filter blocks the change, and the same pattern runs again — the subconscious treats every signal as a fresh alarm, so attention and tension turn the volume up <break time="800ms"/>
Hypnosis takes that same intention and moves it past the filter, straight to the subconscious, where we install the new pattern with positive suggestion: in hypnosis we practice the comfort-dial and soft-body response so the nervous system rehearses ease, supporting the medical plan <break time="700ms"/>
I will work with the eighty-eight percent. You work with the twelve. Together, that is one hundred percent. <break time="600ms"/>
That makes sense, doesn't it? <mark name="await_tom_yes"/><break time="4s"/>
And the way we begin is by testing your suggestibility.</p>
<mark name="stage_induction"/>
<p>Sit upright, feet flat on the floor, and rest your left arm on the table. <break time="600ms"/>
Look at that arm now, from the elbow to the fingertips — take a good picture of it — and close your eyes. <break time="900ms"/>
See that arm and hand in your mind, and when you can remember exactly what they look like, nod your head. <mark name="await_visualize_arm"/><break time="4s"/></p>
<mark name="stage_sugg_questions"/>
<p>Keep the eyes closed and just answer naturally. <break time="500ms"/>
Have you ever walked or talked in your sleep? <mark name="await_q1"/><break time="4s"/>
Have you ever woken in the night feeling unable to move or speak for a moment? <mark name="await_q2"/><break time="4s"/>
After an argument, do you replay what you could have said? <mark name="await_q3"/><break time="4s"/>
If you think of biting into a sour lemon, does your mouth water? <mark name="await_q4"/><break time="4s"/>
Can you picture things clearly in your mind? <mark name="await_q5"/><break time="4s"/></p>
<mark name="stage_physio"/>
<p>Now, as you begin to enter hypnosis, your body will show a series of changes. I will notice them before you do — when you become aware of each one, just nod and let me know. <break time="800ms"/>
First, the breathing tends to grow deeper as the body takes in more oxygen. When you notice your breathing deepening, nod your head. <mark name="await_breath"/><break time="4s"/>
The lips, mouth, and throat may feel a little dry, with an urge to swallow. Completely natural — when you notice it, nod. <mark name="await_swallow"/><break time="4s"/>
And notice the eyes now — that faint fluttering of the eyes beneath the lids. <break time="700ms"/>
That is the first stage of hypnosis.</p>
<mark name="stage_conversion"/>
<p>Let your attention drift to the elbow resting comfortably on the table, down through the forearm, into the wrist, the hand, the fingertips. <break time="700ms"/>
And you might imagine that hand becoming lighter — as if many powerful helium balloons were tied around the wrist — light as a feather, and then even lighter. <break time="900ms"/>
As you allow that, you may notice little pulling or tugging motions beginning — you are creating that sensation, and that gives you a greater sense of control. <break time="900ms"/>
And at the same time you can feel the other hand a little heavier on your leg, the feet against the floor, the rhythm of your breath — and when you notice the light hand and the heavy hand feeling different, you can nod. <mark name="await_weight_diff"/><break time="4s"/>
And it becomes a floating feeling — a tendency to <emphasis level="strong">lift with each breath</emphasis> — rising now, pivoting, the palm turning gently inward — and I wonder whether it feels more like the hand being drawn to the face, or the face to the hand, or both drawn together. I will let you decide. <break time="1200ms"/></p><p>And when the hand comes all the way in to touch the face — the moment you make skin contact — nod your head, and you will reach your peak of suggestibility and enter the hypnotic sleep. <mark name="await_skin_contact"/><break time="4s"/>
<mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1500ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_count_5_0"/>
<p>Gently let the hand come back down to rest. <break time="800ms"/>
Now a count from five down to zero, and each count carries you deeper. <break time="700ms"/>
Five — going down now, deeper into hypnosis. <break time="800ms"/>
Four — the body releasing, relaxing, letting go. <break time="800ms"/>
Three — an emotional calmness. <break time="800ms"/>
Two — the mind relaxing now. <break time="800ms"/>
One — deeper and deeper. <break time="800ms"/>
And zero — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_reactional"/>
<p>In a moment — not yet — I will ask you to open your eyes and fix them on a point above you, and when I say deep sleep, they close again and you go twice as deep. <break time="900ms"/>
Open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> Twice as deep. <break time="1200ms"/>
Open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/>
Open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> Twice as deep. <break time="1200ms"/>
One more time — open your eyes. <break time="2s"/>And — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1500ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_deepener"/>
<p>Picture a level garden path with 10 smooth stepping stones, each one carrying you deeper into calm. <break time="800ms"/>
10 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>9 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>8 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>7 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>6 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>5 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>4 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>3 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>2 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>1 — a soft, steady step, deeper down. <break time="900ms"/>At the last stone you settle onto a comfortable bench, twice as deep.</p> <break time="1500ms"/>
<mark name="stage_prog_relax"/>
<p>Rest back now, feeling the rhythm of your breath, giving yourself permission to let me guide you. <break time="900ms"/>
and you can feel the first wave of softening begin, <break time="500ms"/>You can see, in the mind's eye, a soft light of calm resting at the crown of the head, <break time="500ms"/>you can hear how quiet and even the breath is becoming. <break time="900ms"/>
Bring your attention to the top of the head, and let a wave of relaxation wash down over the scalp, <break time="400ms"/>the forehead, <break time="400ms"/>the temples, <break time="400ms"/>the eyelids and cheeks, loosening the jaw. <break time="800ms"/>
Any outside sounds are only the everyday sounds of life — they cannot distract you; they simply send you deeper. <break time="900ms"/>
The calm flows through the neck and shoulders, softening them, <break time="600ms"/>down the upper arms, the elbows, the forearms, releasing the wrists, the hands, all the way to the fingertips. <break time="900ms"/>
Into the chest now, opening, letting go of anything that no longer serves you, <break time="600ms"/>down through the center, the belly unwinding. <break time="800ms"/>
The breathing deep, gentle, rhythmic — that drowsy, daydream feeling taking over — letting go, drifting down. <break time="900ms"/>
Through the hips, <break time="500ms"/>down the thighs, the large muscles and the small, <break time="500ms"/>the knees, the shins, the calves, <break time="600ms"/>the ankles, the heels, the arches, every toe loose to the very tips. <break time="1000ms"/>
And now a count from five to zero, each count deeper still — five, letting go — four — three — two — one — zero — <mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>And from now on, whenever we are working together for the purpose of hypnosis, and with your permission, the words <emphasis level="strong">deep sleep</emphasis> bring you back to this state quickly, calmly, and deeply, and the body relaxes. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_suggestions"/>
<p>And in this deep, receptive state, your subconscious accepts what serves you. <break time="900ms"/>
You might imagine comfort as a warmth that spreads a little further each time you rest like this. <break time="1100ms"/>
Perhaps the sensation can move to arm's length for a while — noticed, respected, and quieter. <break time="1100ms"/>
And you may find the body remembering how to rest around a signal instead of gripping it. <break time="1100ms"/>
It can be reassuring to know your medical team leads the way, while this skill of ease travels with you. <break time="1100ms"/>
</p>
<p>Now see it. <break time="500ms"/>
Picture a calm room where everyone is at ease — and within that scene, watch yourself living the change — the body unlearns bracing; attention widens; the signal is acknowledged and then turned down like a dial <break time="1200ms"/>
Like steady hands that steady others. <break time="900ms"/>
And feel it — <break time="400ms"/>let the body register how this new pattern feels: a deep, private steadiness, and the quiet certainty that this change lasts. <break time="1200ms"/>
There is no rush — it is unfolding now, naturally, automatically, with each breath. <break time="800ms"/>
And whether you notice the first change tonight, or sometime tomorrow, or in a quiet moment you did not expect — the change is already underway, and you can <emphasis level="moderate">trust yourself now</emphasis>. <break time="900ms"/>
You are not becoming someone new. You are remembering who you have always been. <break time="1200ms"/></p>
<p>In sleep, the body can continue this softening on its own, so rest becomes deeper and mornings easier. <break time="900ms"/></p>
<mark name="stage_emerge"/>
<p>It is time now to come back up, calm and refreshed. I will count from zero to five, and with each number you grow more alert, more awake, with a positive sense of well-being. <break time="900ms"/>
Zero — we close the door to the subconscious, sealing out every negative. <break time="700ms"/>
One — slowly and calmly coming up. <break time="700ms"/>
Two — physically aware, mentally alert. <break time="700ms"/>
Three — coming up higher now. <break time="700ms"/>
Four — the eyes beginning to open, and — <break time="600ms"/>
Five — <emphasis level="strong">eyes open, wide awake.</emphasis> <break time="600ms"/>
One two three four five — eyes open, wide awake.</p>
<mark name="stage_finger_spread"/>
<p>Before we finish, one quick check. Sit with your feet flat and rest your hands on your legs. <break time="600ms"/>
Raise one arm, bend it at the elbow, and hold the palm about ten inches in front of your face, fingers straight, just above eye level. <break time="900ms"/>
Stare at the middle finger. Take a deep breath — and on the exhale, the fingers begin to separate. <break time="900ms"/>
Separating — wider and wider, further and further apart. <break time="900ms"/>
Separating — wider and wider, further and further apart. <break time="900ms"/>
Separating — wider and wider, further and further apart. <break time="1000ms"/>
And as the fingers keep separating — tell me, is it the hand, or the arm, that is starting to pull in toward your face? <mark name="await_hand_or_arm"/><break time="4s"/>
Good. The {limb} keeps pulling in closer and closer as the fingers separate — and when you notice your breathing getting deeper, taking in more oxygen, nod and let me know. <mark name="await_fs_breath"/><break time="4s"/>
When the eyelids start to blink and grow heavy, nod — and the more they blink, the heavier they become, and the heavier they become, the more they blink — so heavy now they would much rather stay closed. <mark name="await_fs_eyes"/><break time="4s"/>
Closer and closer with every breath — and when the hand comes all the way in to touch the face, nod — the moment you make skin contact, the eyes close, and you reach your peak and enter the hypnotic sleep. <mark name="await_fs_contact"/><break time="4s"/>
<mark name="cue_snap"/><emphasis level="strong">Deep sleep.</emphasis> <break time="1500ms"/>
Perfect. <break time="500ms"/>And now — one two three four five — eyes open, wide awake.</p>
<mark name="stage_homework"/>
<p>Before next session: <break time="400ms"/>
Twice daily, five minutes: self-guided comfort-dial practice in the chosen scene. <break time="600ms"/>
Keep the comfort log (0–10, morning and evening) and bring it — the log is shared with Dr. Patel on request. <break time="600ms"/>
Picture next week for a moment — you, walking in, telling me the first win. <break time="600ms"/>
Same time next week, and notice what changes in the meantime.</p>
</prosody>
</speak>